@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-function-comparators Function Comparators
@brief Functions for comparing actual and expected values in tests.

@details
While any boolean expression can be used in expectations and requirements,
dedicated comparator functions such as `eq`, `ne`, `lt`, `le`, `gt`, and
`ge` provide enhanced reporting by explicitly showing the compared values
when a check fails.

Using these comparators ensures that failed checks report both actual and
expected values, making issues easier to diagnose.

Comparators are available for both fundamental types and standard
containers. When a container comparison fails, the elements of both
containers are shown.

@par Examples

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check various conditions", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::eq (compute_answer (), 42)) << "answer is 42";
  t.expect (mt::ne (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer is not 43";
  t.expect (mt::lt (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer is < 43";
  t.expect (mt::le (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer is <= 43";
  t.expect (mt::gt (compute_answer (), 41)) << "answer is > 41";
  t.expect (mt::ge (compute_answer (), 42)) << "answer is >= 42";

  t.expect (compute_condition ()) << "condition is true";
});
@endcode

If a comparator check fails, the output clearly indicates the actual and
expected values. For example, when `compute_answer()` returns 41, the
human reporter displays:

@code{.console}
    • Check various conditions
        ✗ answer is 42 FAILED (main.cpp:21, 41 == 42)
        ✗ answer is > 41 FAILED (main.cpp:25, 41 > 41)
        ✗ answer is >= 42 FAILED (main.cpp:26, 41 >= 42)
    ✗ Check various conditions - FAILED (3 checks passed, 3 failed)
@endcode

The C strings (`char` arrays, including the string literals, and `char`
pointers) are compared by content, also with `std::string` and
`std::string_view`, so `mt::eq (actual, "abc")` checks the characters,
not the addresses; a null `char` pointer is equal only to another null
pointer (or `nullptr`), not to an empty string. The other arrays
(including `uint8_t` buffers) are compared by content, element by
element, also with a container; the other pointers are compared by
address.

The operands must be comparable with the operator of the comparator; for
example, `mt::eq (a, b)` does not compile when `a == b` would not. For the
values of the application types to be displayed in the reports, see
@ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-customisation. The
comparison of integers of different signedness is made by value, as by
`std::cmp_equal()`, so that `mt::eq (-1, static_cast<unsigned> (-1))`
fails, although `-1 == static_cast<unsigned> (-1)` is true.

@par Comparing Approximately

The comparison of floating-point values with `eq()` is exact, so
`mt::eq (0.1 + 0.2, 0.3)` fails. To accept a difference, use
`mt::approx (lhs, rhs, epsilon)`, which is true when the operands differ
by at most the explicit tolerance `epsilon`, the limit included. The
operands and the tolerance are integral or floating-point numbers, of any
type; the distance of integers is computed exactly, also for operands of
different signedness. A negative tolerance fails all the comparisons. A
failed check shows the operands and the tolerance, as in
`1.0 ~ (1.5 +/- 0.1)`.

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check approximate values", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::approx (read_adc (), 512, 3)) << "ADC reading";
  t.expect (mt::approx (compute_float (), 42.0f, 0.001f))
      << "result is about 42";
});
@endcode

@par Comparing Containers

Containers can be compared for equality or inequality using the same
comparator functions. The comparison is performed with the `==`
operator of the containers, so they must be of types that can be
compared, usually of the same type. The standard `==` and `!=` operators
return a plain `bool`, so a failed check written with them could not
show the elements. Only the first 16 elements of a container are shown;
when two longer containers differ after them, the report adds the index
of the first difference.

@par Examples

@code{.cpp}
namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

ts.test ("Check containers", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::eq (std::vector<int>{ 1, 2 }, std::vector<int>{ 1, 2 }))
      << "vector{ 1, 2 } eq vector{ 1, 2 }";

  t.expect (mt::ne (std::vector<int>{ 1, 2, 3 },
                    std::vector<int>{ 1, 2, 4 }))
      << "vector{ 1, 2, 3 } ne vector{ 1, 2, 4 }";
});
@endcode

